theory.intro:
Coordinates name a point on a grid as an ordered pair $(x, y)$. The first number is the horizontal position, the second is the vertical position.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.75]
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (0,0) grid (6,6);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(6.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,6.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \n in {1,...,6}{\node[below,font=\small] at (\n,0) {$\n$};\node[left,font=\small] at (0,\n) {$\n$};}
\node[below left,font=\small] at (0,0) {$0$};
\coordinate (P) at (3.5,4.5);
\draw[dashed] (P)--(3.5,0);
\draw[dashed] (P)--(0,4.5);
\fill (P) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above right,font=\small] at (P) {$P(x,y)$};
\node[font=\small] at (3.5,-1.1) {across first ($x$)};
\node[font=\small,anchor=east] at (-0.7,4.5) {then up ($y$)};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

